<section #sec:inline-code | Inline Code>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Mark a snippet inside running text with backticks, or with the mirrored sigil when you want to tag its language. Both render to an inline `code` element.

<code #code:inline-basic>
Assign with `x = 1`, and call <`python | factorial(n)`> to recurse.
</code>

<minipage #mp:inline-basic>
Assign with `x = 1`, and call <`python | factorial(n)`> to recurse.
</minipage>
</section>

<section #sec:code-blocks | Code Blocks>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A code block holds a verbatim, multi-line listing. Write it with the mirrored triple-backtick sigil — the language goes before the pipe — or with a plain markdown fence.

<code #code:block-basic>
<``` python |
import math
radius = 2.0
area = math.pi * radius ** 2
```>
</code>

<minipage #mp:block-basic>
<``` python |
import math
radius = 2.0
area = math.pi * radius ** 2
```>
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

<table csv -numbered>
language (sets the language-X class for highlighting), python  ·  r  ·  js
Id, #code:area
Class, .extra-class
</table>

<sub-section | Notes>

Content is verbatim: newlines are preserved and nothing inside is read as a tag — backticks, angle brackets, and pipes all pass through untouched.
</section>
